# Diagram Rule

Every important concept in the handbook must include a visual.

- Prefer **Mermaid** diagrams (`flowchart`, `graph`, `sequenceDiagram`) — they
  render in the Cursor/GitHub markdown preview and stay text-diffable
- Fall back to **ASCII art** only when Mermaid can't cleanly express the idea
  (e.g. array index pointers like `[1 2 3 4]` with `^` markers underneath)
- Never assume a diagram is self-explanatory — always add 1-3 sentences of
  prose immediately after it explaining what it shows
- Save reusable Mermaid sources to `assets/diagrams/<pattern-name>.mmd` so they
  can be reused across the cheat sheet and recognition guide
- Keep diagrams focused on one idea each — don't cram an entire algorithm's
  control flow into a single diagram if it can be split into a "before" and
  "after" step

Example of a properly explained diagram:

```markdown
\`\`\`mermaid
flowchart LR
L[left=0] --> W[window] --> R[right]
\`\`\`

The window expands by moving `right` forward, and only shrinks by moving `left`
forward once the window violates the constraint (e.g. contains a duplicate).
```
